<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Aranjuez (1801)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Aranjuez of 21 March 1801 was an agreement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> confirming the secret Treaty of San Ildefonso of 1800. Duke Ferdinand of Parma renounced his duchy, and his son Louis received Tuscany as the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, in return for Spain's retrocession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> to France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aranjuez-1801.jpg" alt="Treaty of Aranjuez (1801)" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Enok · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italia_1796-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire">Coup of 18 Brumaire</a> in November 1799, First Consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and his foreign minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> made colonial expansion a key policy. Napoleon wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> as the hub of a new French empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, to replace the one lost in 1763, and as a source of food and slaves for French plantations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>. Spain's alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had brought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-spanish-war-1796-1808">Anglo-Spanish War</a> of 1798 to 1802 and a British blockade that hurt its economy, while American settlers pressing west made Louisiana costly to hold.</p><p>The Spanish chief minister Manuel Godoy was willing to give up the territory, but asked for six French ships of the line and compensation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to make the deal acceptable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a>. Charles's wife, Maria Luisa of Parma, was the sister of Ferdinand of Parma, whose duchy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had occupied since 1796, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> proposed a kingdom for Ferdinand's younger son Louis. The terms were fixed in the secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-treaty-of-san-ildefonso">Third Treaty of San Ildefonso</a> of October 1800, kept quiet while France negotiated the Convention of 1800 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The terms were vague: the second clause said the new state might be the Grand Duchy of Tuscany, the three Roman legations or other provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> forming a rounded state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in February 1801 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Ferdinand III, Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, exchange his duchy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a>, and in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> of 28 March 1801 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> handed Piombino and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-the-presidi">State of the Presidi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Talleyrand kept the Presidi and joined Piombino to Tuscany to form the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> confirmed the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> after 30,000 French troops landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> in December 1801. That expedition failed, and its leader, General Charles Leclerc, died of yellow fever with an estimated 22,000 of his men. Without Saint-Domingue, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> judged Louisiana irrelevant and in April 1803 sold it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for $15 million. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> was dissolved and joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1807, and the Congress of Vienna restored Tuscany and Parma in 1815.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Aranjuez (1801)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By agreements between France and Spain in 1800–1801, including the <b>Treaty of Aranjuez</b>, the Grand Duchy of Tuscany was taken from its Habsburg-Lorraine ruler and turned into the Kingdom of Etruria for Louis of Bourbon-Parma, in exchange for the Bourbon claim to the Duchy of Parma and Piacenza.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-etruria">Kingdom of Etruria</a></li></ul></section>
